  "text_md": "## Issue Number 1, 2006\n\n## <u>IMMEDIATE PAST PRESIDENT’S MESSAGE</u>\n\n|life to Carlsbad, California.|I have some|\n|---|---|\n|family in the area and had met a friend||\n|there last year.||\n|and an avid photographer.|But the|\n\nDear OPCC members,\n\nMy last five years as a member of OPCC have been very enjoyable and rewarding. I have met many talented people and have made many friends, and have learned more about photography than I had hoped for. **________________________________________________________________________**\n\n## <u>SENSOR DUST</u>\n\nBob Huelsdonk\n\nLast January, I submitted a digital projected competition print from my digital Canon D60 SLR which showed some wind generators against a plain blue sky. Two of our discerning members, Jean Timmermeister and Valerie Henschel commented that the sky had a dim but noticeable dust spot on it. I had not noticed it\n\nThere have been a lot of changes in my life in the last two years, and I have decided to move from western Washington where I have lived all my\n\n## She is a world traveler\n\n## Northwest will always be my \"home\".\n\nThe travel bug is still with me having traveled to Europe, Africa, South America and Antarctica since we last met and with a few weeks in Southeast Asia scheduled for February.\n\nI will send Jim my email address once I get settled so we can keep in contact. Thanks again for the friendships and I wish all of you a Great Christmas and all the best in the New Year.\n\n## Bob Mandich\n\non my computer screen or even on the projected image until it was pointed out.\n\nReturning home, I put the print on my computer screen. Indeed it showed as about a 1/2 inch, slightly darker blob (on an 8x10 image). I then increased the contrast greatly, and lo and\n",
